  "image_name": "4fbb56170a406c7408b08c82f0fc4bad.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the clinical course of fatty liver disease as a dynamic, partly reversible process rather than a simple one-way sequence. It begins with steatosis, then progresses through steatosis with lobular inflammation to NASH, followed by early fibrosis, advanced fibrosis with compensated cirrhosis, decompensated cirrhosis, and finally HCC.\n\nA key message is that early disease stages involving fat accumulation and inflammation can shift back and forth, shown by paired forward red arrows and reverse blue arrows. Fibrosis progression is also depicted as non-linear: patients may move across fibrosis stages F1 to F4 at different rates, with “rapid progressors” and “slow progressors” highlighted. This suggests multiple possible trajectories rather than a uniform timeline.\n\nLater stages emphasize worsening structural liver damage, but the diagram also indicates some potential for “recompensation” after decompensated cirrhosis, at least in NAFLD, with a blue arrow pointing back toward compensated disease. At the top, a long arrow labeled “multiple parallel hits” implies several simultaneous drivers of disease progression, while a large blue arrow along the bottom stresses the possibility of reversing the underlying pathophysiology, especially before end-stage disease.",
